.th FORTRAN I 12/17/75
.sh NAME
fortran \*- fortran IV compiler
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd fortran
[ flags ] file ...
.sh DESCRIPTION
Fortran is a new fortran iv compiler for the UNIX
time-sharing system.
Arguments may be either files or flags;
flags begin with a '-'. The valid flags are:
.s3
.ft I
.lp +9 8
flag	meaning
.ft R
.s3
.lp +9 8
-db	Compile statements with a `d' in column 1. 
Usually they are treated as comments.
.s3
.lp +9 8
-80	Take a full 80 columns of source.
Usually only
columns 1-72 are used.
.s3
.lp +9 8
-ls:n	Listing control. The listing file is enabled.
Its contents are determined by the sum of the
following modes:
.br
.nf
			0 = list errors
			1 = list source
			2 = list storage map
			4 = list (pseudo) generated code
.br
.fi
E.g. -ls:3 directs that source and storage map
information be placed on the listing file.
.s3
.lp +9 8
-ot	Print the options-in-effect table
.s3
.lp +9 8
-no	Suppress object code output
.s3
.lp +9 8
-lr:n	Set run-time logical record size to n, 10<_n<_4096
The default is 136.
This is only valid if a main
program is being compiled.
.s3
.lp +9 8
-sn	Suppress run-time code to record statement
number (isn's)
.s3
.lp +9 8
-i4	Set the default integer precision to 4 bytes.
.s3
.lp +9 8
-nv	Disable vectoring of arrays.
Since all UNIX systems have hardware multiply,
array vectoring is usually of minimal advantage.
.s3
.lp +9 8
-wr	Print warning messages
.s3
.lp +9 8
-xs:n	Allocate an extra n Kw of core for the compiler.
The initial allocation is 6Kw, which is more
than sufficient for almost all programs.
.s3
.lp +9 8
-st	Print statistics summary at end of compilation
.s3
.lp +9 8
-ck	Generate code to check subscripts at run-time.
This is a no-op now.
.s3
.lp +9 8
-op	Enable optimization.
.i0
.dt
.s3
.ul
File control:
.s3
Fortran may have up to 8 input files.
If an input file
cannot be opened, the suffix ".for" is appended and another
attempt is made.
If no listing file is explicitly specified,
and the -ls option is not used, no listing file is created.
Otherwise, the name of the listing file may be explicitly stated
by:
.s3
		-l=filename
.br
or,
		-l=
.s3
if the listing is to be produced to the standard output.
If no
filename is specified, the name of the first source file with the
suffix ".lst" is used.
Similarly, the object file name may be specified
by
.s3
		-o=filename
.s3
If no object file is specified, the name of the first source file
with the suffix ".obj" is used.
.s3
.ul
Example:
.s3
The following command line compiles the fortran main program
in mainprog.for, and the subroutines in sub1.for ... sub3.for,
places the output in the file tolink.obj, and the listing on the
standard output file (the keyboard).
Lots of options are invoked:
.s3
%fortran -o=tolink.obj -l= -ls:3 -wr -ck mainprog sub1 sub2 sub3
.s3
